course: biology
lesson: 103
title: The Australian walkabout: kangaroos, emus, wallabies and dingoes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In the Australian walkabout there is no fence between you and the animals: you walk along a path while kangaroos doze in the shade a few steps away, and the keepers only ask you to stay on the path and let the animals be.
explain: A kangaroo is a living pogo stick: springy tendons in its back legs store energy at every landing, so it bounces along fast for very little effort, and a joey peeps out of its mother's pouch to watch.
explain: When a kangaroo moves slowly it leans on its tail as well as its paws and feet, so the tail works like a fifth leg, and two males fighting lean on their tails to kick.
explain: Skippy, Roo from Winnie the Pooh and cartoon boxers in gloves make kangaroos look like cuddly toys, but a real male's kick is rough, and the emu, the cassowary and the dingo are wild animals to watch from a safe distance.
fact: In a walkabout visitors walk among kangaroos and wallabies on paths with no fence, and the kangaroos rest in the heat of the day and graze at dawn and dusk.
fact: Kangaroos hop with springy tendons that store energy like a pogo stick, so hopping fast costs them hardly more energy than hopping slowly.
fact: Kangaroos cannot easily walk backwards, and people often say this is why the kangaroo, an animal that goes forward, is on Australia's coat of arms.
fact: A newborn kangaroo is about the size of a jelly bean; it crawls into the pouch and grows there for months on milk, and a mother can have a tiny baby, a joey in the pouch and an older one at foot all at once.
fact: Male kangaroos «box» by pushing with their arms and kicking with their back legs while leaning on their tails, to win rank, and the kicks can be dangerous.
fact: The emu is Australia's biggest bird; it cannot fly, runs at about 50 kilometres per hour, booms from a throat sac, and the father sits on the eggs for about eight weeks, hardly eating or drinking.
fact: In the «Emu War» of 1932 soldiers with machine guns tried to stop emus eating farmers' wheat and mostly failed, because the emus ran fast in scattered groups.
fact: Wallabies are smaller relatives of kangaroos; rock wallabies climb cliffs, and some wallabies brought to Britain and New Zealand now live wild there.
fact: The quokka is a small wallaby from islands near Perth whose face shape looks like a smile; visitors may not touch or feed quokkas, because fed quokkas can get ill and lose their fear of people, and can be fined for it.
fact: The cassowary of northern Australia and New Guinea is a big flightless bird with a dagger-like claw on its inner toe; its kick can badly hurt people, and by eating fruit it spreads the seeds of many rainforest trees.
fact: Dingoes are wild dogs that came to Australia with people thousands of years ago; they howl rather than bark, and a fence more than 5000 kilometres long keeps them away from sheep farms.
fact: In the bushfires of 2019 and 2020 hot, dry forests burned so fast that many animals could not escape; the fires were reported to have killed or harmed huge numbers of animals, and zoo hospitals such as Taronga and Australia Zoo treated burnt koalas and kangaroos.
exercise: What do keepers ask you to do in the walkabout? | Stay on the path and let the animals be | Feed the kangaroos bread from your hand | Pick up a joey for a photo | Chase them so they hop for you
exercise: When do kangaroos mostly graze? | At dawn and dusk | Only in the middle of a hot day | Only in the dark at midnight | All day without resting
exercise: What makes kangaroo hopping so cheap in energy? | Springy tendons store energy like a pogo stick | Their strong tails push them along like a motor | They are hollow and very light | They only ever hop downhill
exercise: Why do people say the kangaroo is on Australia's coat of arms? | It cannot easily walk backwards, only forward | It is the fastest animal in the world | It was the first animal born in Australia | It once saved the life of the first prime minister
exercise: About how big is a newborn kangaroo? | About the size of a jelly bean | About the size of a fat pet cat | About the size of a football | About the size of an apple
exercise: How many young can a mother kangaroo care for at once? | A tiny baby, a pouch joey and one at foot | Only ever one at a time | A whole litter of six tiny babies in the pouch | None, the father raises them
exercise: How do male kangaroos fight? | They push with their arms and kick, leaning on their tails | They only bite each other's ears and tails, never kicking | They head-butt with horns | They spit at each other from far away
exercise: How does an emu make its deep booming sound? | With a sac in its throat | By stamping on the ground | By clapping its wings together | By banging its beak on wood
exercise: Who sits on emu eggs? | The father, for about eight weeks | The mother, for about eight short days | Nobody, the hot sand keeps them warm | Both parents, taking turns of an hour each
exercise: What happened in the «Emu War» of 1932? | Soldiers tried to stop emus eating wheat and mostly failed | Emus attacked a big city and were chased away by the army | Farmers trained emus to guard sheep | Emus were moved by ship to New Zealand
exercise: What is a wallaby? | A smaller relative of the kangaroo | A baby kangaroo just out of the pouch | A kind of big Australian wild rabbit | A kangaroo that has lost the power to hop
exercise: Why does a quokka seem to smile? | The shape of its face looks like a smile | It is happy to see visitors | It has been taught by tourists to pose | It is chewing a sweet
exercise: What makes a cassowary dangerous? | A dagger-like claw it kicks with | A poisonous bite like a snake's fangs | It spits burning acid at people's eyes | It can fly down on people from trees
exercise: How do cassowaries help the rainforest? | They spread the seeds of fruit they eat | They dig ponds for frogs | They eat up all the poisonous plants in the forest | They keep the trees short
exercise: What is a dingo? | A wild dog that came with people long ago | A kind of fox from Australia | A pet dog that ran away from its owners last year | A small wolf from Europe
exercise: Why is there a fence over 5000 kilometres long in Australia? | To keep dingoes away from sheep farms | To stop kangaroos crossing roads | To keep the hungry emus out of all the big cities | To mark the border with New Zealand
test: «gonna give that kangaroo some of my crisps» What should you do? | Don't; stay on the path and let it be | Fine, kangaroos love salty snacks like crisps | Fine, if you hold the bag still | Throw them, so it doesn't bite
test: In a cartoon a kangaroo puts on boxing gloves and fights for fun. What is real? | Males push and kick to win rank, and the kicks can really hurt | Kangaroos never fight at all | Kangaroos fight only with their teeth, biting each other's necks | Only females fight, by bumping heads
test: A kangaroo walks slowly on its two front paws, two back feet and its tail. How many parts touch the ground? | 5 | 4 | 3 | 6
test: 6 kangaroos lie in the shade. How many ears and tails altogether? | 18 | 12 | 24 | 6
test: «a joey's born the size of a puppy and jumps in the pouch» What is true? | It is about jelly-bean size and crawls into the pouch | Yes, it is about the size of a puppy, and it jumps in | It hatches from an egg in the pouch | It is born already able to hop
test: «emus won a war? you're having me on» What is right? | In 1932 soldiers failed to stop emus eating wheat | It is just a made-up story from an old cartoon | Emus helped soldiers win a real war | Emus fought against kangaroos
test: «that dingo's just a lost dog, let's whistle it over» What is true? | Dingoes have lived wild for thousands of years and are not pets | Yes, it is someone's pet that ran off | Yes, dingoes love to be stroked and fed by visitors like pets | Dingoes are foxes, not dogs
test: 2 emus and 3 cassowaries walk past. How many legs? | 10 | 20 | 5 | 12
test: What would happen if lots of people fed the quokkas every day? | They could get ill and lose fear of people, so it is banned | They would grow into kangaroos | Nothing, because quokkas only eat leaves anyway and ignore the food | They would learn to smile more
test: «look, a baby kangaroo» says your brother about a small adult. What could it really be? | A wallaby, a smaller kangaroo relative | A dingo puppy that has learned to hop about | A young emu with its feathers fluffed up | A wombat standing up on its back legs
test: Why did so many animals suffer in the 2019 and 2020 bushfires? | Hot, dry forests burned very fast and many could not escape | Fires only burned in the zoos | The animals walked into the fire on purpose | Rain put the fires out too slowly, so the animals got lost in the smoke
test: «cassowary's just a big turkey, I'll shoo it off» Why is that a bad idea? | It can kick hard with a dagger-like claw | Cassowaries can fly at you | Turkeys bite and so do cassowaries | It is fine, they are very shy and never kick
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the locomotion of the kangaroo? | Elastic tendons store energy, so that rapid hopping costs scarcely more than slow hopping | The tail alone propels the animal at great speed | The animal moves backwards as readily as forwards | Hopping requires greater effort at each increase of speed
exam: Identify the condition of the newborn kangaroo at birth. | It is approximately the size of a jelly bean and completes its growth within the pouch | It is approximately the size of a domestic cat and walks at once beside its mother | It hatches from an egg laid upon the ground | It is born fully furred and capable of hopping
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the military operation of 1932 known as the «Emu War». | Soldiers armed with machine guns largely failed to reduce the emus devouring the wheat crop | The emus were successfully removed from all farms within a week | The emus were enlisted to assist the army | The operation was conducted solely in New Zealand
exam: State the principal purpose of the fence extending more than five thousand kilometres across Australia. | To exclude dingoes from the sheep-farming regions | To prevent kangaroos from crossing the highways at night | To confine emus to the coast | To mark the boundary between two states
